# Project: Custom CAD (ALP CAD) based on Sweet Home 3D

## Context
- This is a large Java codebase (Sweet Home 3D) that I am refactoring and extending into my own custom CAD application (ALP CAD).
- I already have a complete implementation roadmap and approximately 18 markdown documents that contain feasibility studies, architecture findings, required features, and design decisions.
- These documents live in the project (usually under /docs, /planning, or similar folders). Always locate and consult them before making significant decisions.

## Coding Standards
- Strictly follow the existing Sweet Home 3D architecture, coding style, naming conventions, and package structure.
- Prefer minimal, targeted changes over large rewrites.
- Respect the MVC pattern, existing model classes, controllers, and 3D rendering approach.
- Keep backward compatibility in mind unless the roadmap explicitly says otherwise.
- When adding new features, integrate them cleanly into the existing plugin/extension points where possible.

## Workflow Rules
1. Before writing any code, confirm which part of the roadmap you are executing and reference the relevant markdown documents.
2. For multi-file changes, first list the exact files you plan to modify and why.
3. Make changes in small, reviewable steps. After each logical group of changes, summarize what was done and wait for approval before continuing.
4. When build, Ant, or runtime changes are needed, provide the exact terminal commands for macOS.
5. Whenever manual testing is required, update the development application bundle so I can test the features by running:
   `/Users/billfranklin/Documents/ChatGPT/Architecture Landscape CAD App/source/SweetHome3D-7.5-src/install/Sweet Home 3D Dev.app`

## Model Selection Strategy (Cost-Conscious)
- Default / everyday work → **Composer 2.5** (uses the generous Cursor Models pool).
- Complex multi-file, architectural, or tricky tasks → **Grok 4.6** (still uses the Cursor Models pool).
- Only use third-party models (Gemini, Claude, GPT, etc.) when Composer or Grok are not sufficient.
- Use Auto (Cost or Balance) freely for light questions and exploration.
- Goal: Burn the generous Cursor Models pool first and protect the $20 Other Models credit.

## Important Constraints
- Cost-conscious: Keep context efficient. Do not re-read the entire codebase unnecessarily.
- Always check the existing implementation roadmap and markdown docs before proposing new approaches.
- Help with compiling, running, and debugging the Java/Ant project when asked.